AI Clinical Trial Card: Navigating the Jagged Technological Frontier: Field Experimental Evidence of the Effects of AI on Knowledge Worker Productivity and Quality

Authors: Fabrizio Dell’Acqua, Raffaella Sadun, Marco Tabellini, and colleagues

AI Model Details

Provider: OpenAI
Model Name and Version: GPT-4 (version available via OpenAI API in late April 2023)

Access Method:

GPT-4 was accessed through a custom company platform developed by the firm

The platform made API calls to OpenAI and surfaced model outputs in a controlled interface used by participants

Fine-tuning:

No fine-tuning reported

Model used in an out-of-the-box configuration

Prompting / Configuration Parameters:

Default system prompt and default temperature

Some experimental conditions provided participants with an overview of GPT-4’s strengths and limitations, but the underlying model configuration remained unchanged

Performance on Standard Benchmarks:

Reported in the paper to contextualize model capability:

MMLU: 86.4% (5-shot)

HellaSwag: 95.3% (10-shot)

HumanEval: 67.0% (0-shot)

Human Participant Profile

Data Collection Period:

2023

Sample Size:

758 consultants

385 participants assigned to tasks “inside the frontier”

373 participants assigned to tasks “outside the frontier”

Population / Expertise:

Professional management consultants at Boston Consulting Group (BCG)

Participants are highly skilled knowledge workers with substantial domain expertise

Domain Expertise:

Business strategy, marketing, and analytical problem-solving

AI / LLM Familiarity:

Measured via surveys

Study conducted ~6 months after the public release of ChatGPT, when general familiarity with LLMs was still relatively low

Training / Onboarding:

Participants received task instructions

Some conditions included an overview describing GPT-4’s capabilities and limitations, intended to calibrate expectations

Interaction and Task Design

Task Description:

Two classes of tasks:

Inside-the-frontier tasks: creative and ideation-heavy consulting tasks (e.g., generating product ideas for a beverage or footwear company)

Outside-the-frontier tasks: structured, quantitative tasks (e.g., spreadsheet-based data analysis)

Interface:

Custom company interface integrating GPT-4 responses

AI Role / Degree of Autonomy:

Assistive

GPT-4 provided suggestions, ideas, and written content; humans retained full control over final outputs

Experimental Conditions:

No AI (control)

GPT-4 only

GPT-4 + Overview (participants received guidance about model strengths/limits)

Primary Outcome Measures:

Quality of output, assessed by human graders

Graders included BCG professionals and MBA students at a top business school

Quality score averaged across 18 evaluation dimensions

Main Effects:

For tasks inside the frontier:

GPT-4 + Overview increased quality scores by 1.75 points over a control mean of 4.1 (~42.5% increase)

GPT-4 only increased scores by 1.56 points (~38% increase)

For tasks outside the frontier:

No productivity or quality gains; in some cases, performance declined

Notes on Interpretation

This study highlights that model capability relative to task demands is a first-order determinant of human–AI outcomes

Even highly skilled professionals benefit from AI assistance when tasks align with model strengths

Conversely, AI can hinder performance when applied outside its effective domain

The inclusion of an “overview” condition demonstrates that meta-knowledge about AI can meaningfully shape outcomes, even when the model itself is unchanged